// bindgen: emitted 163 declarations (7 enum, 8 type, 0 const, 148 fn); skipped 0; 498 declaration(s) the config's allowlist does not ask for.
// bindgen:end declarations

// ── The seven public enums are the generator's now ─────────────────────────
//
// They were 151 values transcribed out of `raylib.h` by hand, in a block right
// here, until CP-1b — the last vendored binding that still did it. They are
// `foreign enum`s inside the block above now: Dado declares the names, C
// supplies every number, and `bindgen.toml`'s `[types.rename]` and
// `[types.enum_prefix]` are where `key.A` is said. A renumbered enumerator
// upstream is no longer an event; a renamed one is `cc`'s error, by name.
//
// **A member is a distinct foreign type, where it used to be a plain integer,
// and the parameters that take one say so.** The old block was a *structural*
// Dado enum, so `IsKeyDown(key.LEFT)` needed no conversion — `IsKeyDown` takes
// `int` in `raylib.h` and a structural member *was* one. A `foreign enum` is
// distinct, which for a while made that call `IsKeyDown(i32(key.LEFT))`. Now
// `bindgen.toml`'s `[declarations.param_enums]` writes the
// nineteen parameters raylib documents as an enum with the enum's type —
// `bool IsKeyDown(key key)` — so the call is `IsKeyDown(key.LEFT)` again, and a
// key code cannot be passed where a mouse button goes. The header still says
// `int`, and the `_Generic` assertion over each signature accepts that: a
// `foreign enum` at a parameter is checked as the enum or as its backing,
// `#c.int` when the declaration writes none. `GetKeyPressed` and
// `GetGamepadButtonPressed` still answer `i32` — a mapping is a parameter's.

// What raylib costs at the link step.
//
// This was a `link` file beside the source until 2026-09-01.
// The objection to the file was never that it was untidy: the `#DARWIN:` /
// `#LINUX:` selectors it carried were a **format invented to stand in for
// `when`**, matched by string against `#OS`'s member names by a reader that
// went a whole milestone without being written at all — and while it was
// unwritten every conditional line in this file silently did nothing. The
// clause below is folded by the compile-time branch the language already has,
// so a mistyped `#LNIUX` is a diagnostic instead of a dropped flag.
//
// The clause-only form (a `foreign` line with no `:` and no body) is what lets
// a `when` carry one line instead of eight hundred: `raylib.h` is already
// declared above, and naming it again here adds no second `#include`.
//
// There is no `-L` on either platform: `brew install raylib` and a distro's
// `raylib-dev` both put the library where the linker already looks, and
// `./dado` adds `brew --prefix`'s lib directory besides.
//
// The BSDs are deliberately absent. raylib builds there and the flags are
// close to Linux's, but they are untested here, and a guessed list fails at
// the linker with a message about the guess rather than about the gap.
when #OS == #DARWIN:
    // The platform backend is Cocoa, input is IOKit, the display link is
    // CoreVideo. Not merely unnecessary on Linux — `-framework` is not an
    // option gcc has at all, so these must be conditional or the build dies
    // before the linker is reached.
    foreign "raylib.h" link "-framework Cocoa -framework IOKit -framework CoreVideo"
else when #OS == #LINUX:
    // GL for the renderer, X11 for the window, and the four libc companions a
    // static libraylib pulls in.
    foreign "raylib.h" link "-lGL -lm -lpthread -ldl -lrt -lX11"
